225
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:33,140
The wicked far outnumber the righteous so that justice has become perverted.

226
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:36,160
So Habakkuk comes to God with this complaint.

227
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:39,400
And he says, how long will you let injustice go unpunished?

228
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:45,800
How long are you going to allow your people to rebel against you and to do whatever they want, however they want?

229
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:48,520
How long are you going to let this go on?

230
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:55,500
And I love that he doesn't really give us what most typical prophets in the Old Testament give us.

231
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:59,920
Most typical prophets give us like an introduction to who they are and when they're prophesying.

232
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:03,760
Like, so-and-so was the prophet during these days, and this person was the king.

233
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:05,400
Habakkuk just gets right to it.

234
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:08,140
God, how long are you going to let this go on?

235
00:14:08,140 --> 00:14:20,820
And I think it's cool that we don't have that context because I think what it subtly communicates to the reader is don't worry about, like, don't compare your circumstances to other people's circumstances.

236
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:28,140
Like, if you've got a frustration, a difficulty, a complaint with God, like, just bring it directly to God.

237
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:32,260
It doesn't necessarily matter what the context is in Habakkuk's day.

238
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:37,320
The reality is he's got a complaint against God, and he's going to bring it to God.

239
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:47,800
Oftentimes, I think if we're honest, when we have difficulties, when we have frustrations, when we have complaints, rather than taking it directly to God, we look around at other people's circumstances.

240
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:52,980
And we compare our circumstances to their circumstances, and it keeps us.

241
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:56,540
It's like a tactic of the enemy to keep us from going directly to God.

242
00:14:56,540 --> 00:15:06,820
Rather than going directly to God with our questions and our concerns and our frustrations, we tend to sit back and to play the comparison game and to say, well, man, it must be nice for them.

243
00:15:06,900 --> 00:15:08,160
They don't have to deal with this.

244
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:21,520
And I think with Habakkuk not giving us context for his issues, it just reminds us that the proper response to pain is not to compare ourselves to other people's pain, but to bring our pain to God.

245
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:33,440
I heard the great theologian, this is not in my notes, the great theologian Joe Rogan say yesterday that the most painful thing that happened to you is the most painful thing that happened to you.

246
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:40,740
Even if you're in here and you've had terrible trauma and difficulty and pain, that's your experience.

247
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:49,420
Some of you have had a really easy life, and maybe you've only had minor difficulties, but that's still very painful to you because your context is things are okay, things are good.

248
00:15:49,420 --> 00:15:56,780
And so wherever you might find yourself on the pain cycle, you shouldn't compare yourself to others and be like, well, I don't have it as bad as them, so I shouldn't complain.

249
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:02,600
No, if you're experiencing pain, bring that pain to God, no matter what it might look like.

250
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:14,140
So if you find yourself in a situation where you don't understand and things seem unjust, it's okay to be sad or angry, but let's bring those emotions to God.

251
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:16,900
That's what the deconstruction series is all about, right?

252
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:17,760
We said it's okay.

253
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:21,940
Like, no question is off limits if you take that question to God.

254
00:16:21,940 --> 00:16:28,800
But if you're going to sit back and complain and argue and not be honest with God, then that's a problem.

255
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:34,280
We tend to make accusations about God without taking them to God.

256
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:38,140
And so Habakkuk just skips that step, and he goes directly to God.

257
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:41,680
He says, how long are you going to let an injustice reign?

258
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:47,720
And God responds in verse 5, awesomely, like usual.

259
00:16:47,720 --> 00:16:50,840
It says, the Lord responds, look around at the nations.

260
00:16:50,840 --> 00:17:00,780
Look and be amazed, for I am about to do something in your day, something that you wouldn't believe even if someone told you about it.

261
00:17:00,780 --> 00:17:04,400
So at this point, if you're Habakkuk, you're like, sweet.

262
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:07,880
My God is going to listen to my complaint.

263
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:13,240
If he's going to do something about it, if you're like me, you might even begin to be quoting Scripture.

264
00:17:13,240 --> 00:17:21,760
In your mind right now, like that passage that says, like, God is able to do far exceedingly abundantly more than we could ever ask or imagine.

265
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:26,540
Habakkuk's probably sitting back like, God is about to do something awesome in my day.

266
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:30,380
But that's not what he says next.

267
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:35,360
He says, I am going to raise up the Babylonians, a cruel and violent people.

268
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:39,460
They will march across the world and conquer other lands.

269
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:43,220
They are notorious for their cruelty and they do whatever they like.

270
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:47,020
Their horses are swifter than cheetahs and fiercer than wolves at dusk.

271
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:50,020
Their charioteers charge from far away like eagles.

272
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:51,940
They swoop down to devour their prey.

273
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:54,380
They come all bent on violence.

274
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:59,280
Hordes advance like a desert wind, sweeping captives ahead of them like sand.

275
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:07,800
And verse 11 says, they sweep past like the wind and are gone, but they are deeply guilty for their own strength is their God.

276
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:14,400
So God responds to Habakkuk's complaint, how are you going to let injustice reign in our day?

277
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:16,960
And God says, don't worry, I have a plan.

278
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:19,840
I am going to raise up the Babylonians.

279
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:26,680
I'm going to raise up this cruel and wicked nation and I'm going to allow them to execute judgment on you.

280
00:18:26,680 --> 00:18:31,680
I'm going to let them execute judgment on the injustice that's happening in our day.

281
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:37,040
And if we're honest, something about this response doesn't sit well with us.

282
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:43,980
How can God let unjust people execute his judgment?

283
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:48,780
How can he let a worse nation execute judgment on a less worse nation?

284
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:53,560
And the reality is, when we think about how God interacts with us in our day,

285
00:18:53,560 --> 00:19:01,040
our judgment around justice is often based on us being the center of the story of humanity.

286
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:05,700
Like America in 2021 is the center of God's eternal plan.

287
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:09,080
And so we look around and say, how can you be doing this?

288
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:11,180
How can you be letting this happen this way?

289
00:19:11,180 --> 00:19:12,700
How can you let people get away with this?

290
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:16,460
And the reality is, God's plans are executed on an eternal timeline.

291
00:19:16,460 --> 00:19:23,260
And so sometimes we get tempted to look just at like this one year, this one month, this one week in our life,

292
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:25,040
and we say, God, how can you let this happen?

293
00:19:25,040 --> 00:19:29,820
If you're a good God, if you're faithful, how can you execute judgment in this way?

294
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:35,980
And the reality is, like, let me just give us a message that hopefully will let us breathe out this morning.

295
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:39,260
We're not the center of human history.

296
00:19:39,260 --> 00:19:44,240
Like, I think America needs to get that through our thick brains.

297
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:47,420
Like, we think that we are the center of human history.

298
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:49,160
We think we're the greatest thing ever.

299
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:53,100
And the reality is, Jesus is the center of human history.

300
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:57,760
God's plan of redemption centers on the first and second coming of Jesus.

301
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:02,060
And so he executes his plans and his judgments on an eternal timeline.

302
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:06,280
And it's easy for us to get tempted, to get sucked into our day and age.

303
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:12,940
But I think God's trying to get Habakkuk to step outside of his little sphere of influence and say,

304
00:20:12,940 --> 00:20:17,120
I'm, don't worry, I'm working, I'm moving, I'm going to bring about justice.

305
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:21,460
But it might not always look the way that you want it to look.

306
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:25,940
And Habakkuk responds the same way you or I would respond,

307
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:31,240
which is nice to know that a prophet of God is responding the same way that we would respond.

308
00:20:31,240 --> 00:20:32,820
And he says in verse 12,

309
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:39,700
O Lord, my Holy One, surely you do not plan to wipe us out, not your people, not the nation of Israel,

310
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:43,960
because you're a pure God and you cannot stand the sight of evil.

311
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:46,360
Are you going to wink at the treachery of Babylon?

312
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:51,440
Should you be silent while the wicked swallow up people more righteous than they?

313
00:20:51,440 --> 00:20:53,780
You hear what he's saying here?

314
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:59,340
He's saying, God, you can't take a more evil nation and execute judgment on an evil nation.

315
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:09,220
Like, he's building out this scale and he's saying, God, how can a holy God use a more unjust nation to judge a less unjust nation?

316
00:21:09,220 --> 00:21:12,560
And then I love what he says at the beginning of chapter 2 in verse 1.

317
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:18,020
He brings this complaint to God and then he says, I'm going to climb up in my watchtower.

318
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:26,340
I'm going to stand at my guard post and I will wait to see what the Lord has to say and how he will answer my complaints.

319
00:21:26,340 --> 00:21:33,120
See, we love to tell God how we would do things if we were him.

320
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:43,580
And oftentimes when we do that, we tend to judge on a curve that we design to make ourselves look better than we actually are.

321
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:49,360
Like, we love to come to God and say, God, if I were you, this is what I would do.

322
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,420
If I were you, this is how I would see things.

323
00:21:51,420 --> 00:21:54,280
And what he says to God is, Israel, yes, we're bad.

324
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:55,640
We're an unjust nation.

325
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:57,080
We do bad and evil things.

326
00:21:57,080 --> 00:21:59,180
But we're not near as evil as the Babylonians.

327
00:21:59,180 --> 00:22:03,420
You can't use the Babylonians to execute your judgment on us.

328
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:05,200
How often do we do this?

329
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:06,520
Like, God, yeah, I know.

330
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:07,600
Like, I haven't been perfect.

331
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:08,500
I've made some mistakes.

332
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:09,100
But them?

333
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:10,880
You're going to let them get the promotion?

334
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:13,300
You're going to let them have the child?

335
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:16,480
You're going to let them reap that blessing or that benefit?

336
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:19,460
Like, I know I'm not perfect, but I'm better than them.

337
00:22:19,460 --> 00:22:21,560
Nobody's ever done that before.

338
00:22:21,560 --> 00:22:23,480
Nobody's ever said that before, right?

339
00:22:23,480 --> 00:22:26,000
And that's where Habakkuk finds himself.

340
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,180
He says, this can't happen.

341
00:22:28,180 --> 00:22:30,920
You can't be doing this this way.

342
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,260
This is not real justice.

343
00:22:33,260 --> 00:22:34,440
You're a good God.

344
00:22:34,440 --> 00:22:36,980
You can't let sin go unpunished.

345
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:41,120
But then I love that he brings his complaint to God.

346
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:46,200
And then he waits and listens for God's response.

347
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:50,020
He says, I will climb up on my watchtower.

348
00:22:50,020 --> 00:22:51,340
I will stand in my guard post.

349
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:56,440
There I will wait to see what the Lord says and how he will answer my complaint.

350
00:22:56,440 --> 00:23:00,800
Again, I told you at the beginning, this is one of my favorite books.

351
00:23:00,800 --> 00:23:02,720
I've read this over and over and over again.

352
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:06,820
But this verse, chapter 2, verse 1, hit differently for me this year

353
00:23:06,820 --> 00:23:08,800
because I picked up a new hobby recently.

354
00:23:09,980 --> 00:23:16,060
I started hunting, which is weird for me because I'm a lover of all things furry and sweet.

355
00:23:16,060 --> 00:23:18,040
But, you know, I decided.

356
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:20,060
There's a lot of reasons why.

357
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:22,660
But I decided to try and get into this thing.

358
00:23:22,660 --> 00:23:26,320
And recently, like, I've been up in tree stands for the first time ever in my life.

359
00:23:27,400 --> 00:23:30,500
And it kind of illustrates to me what Habakkuk's doing here.

360
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:31,780
He's saying, I'm going to go up.

361
00:23:31,780 --> 00:23:34,140
I'm going to wait for the Lord to respond.

362
00:23:34,140 --> 00:23:34,940
I'm going to watch.

363
00:23:34,940 --> 00:23:35,760
I'm going to listen.

364
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:42,120
And when you're up in a tree stand, like, every little sound you hear, you're paying attention to.

365
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:43,100
You're observing.

366
00:23:43,100 --> 00:23:49,060
You're putting this information into your brain because this information might help you get to the result that you want to get to.

367
00:23:51,060 --> 00:24:00,320
And so I think as believers, if we're going to come to God with questions and struggles, we've got to learn to listen.

368
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:10,700
I feel like we are a generation of people that are bringing our frustrations and our complaints to God.

369
00:24:10,700 --> 00:24:15,160
And we already have kind of a hypothetical answer in the back of our minds, and we're not letting him talk.

370
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:19,920
Like, we're never silent enough to actually let God speak.

371
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:24,000
We're filling our minds with information all the time.

372
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:36,740
And sometimes the best thing that we can do is come into our time of prayer with God and lay our requests, our complaints, our frustrations, our doubts, our fears, whatever it might be, lay it all before the Lord.

373
00:24:36,740 --> 00:24:39,240
And then just sit back and listen.

374
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:45,460
Sit back and wait for God to respond.

375
00:24:45,460 --> 00:24:53,120
See, I used to think that chapter 2, verse 1 was Habakkuk kind of arrogantly saying, like, I'm right, you're wrong, I'm going to sit here and wait.

376
00:24:54,000 --> 00:25:00,540
But I think actually what he's communicating is, like, sometimes God speaks in all kinds of different ways.

377
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:06,120
And if we're not intentional to sit and listen, we might miss it all together.

378
00:25:06,120 --> 00:25:17,160
We might bring our complaints, our frustrations, our questions to God, and he might speak back to us, but we might be so busy, so distracted that we're not actually hearing his response.

379
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:20,940
And so Habakkuk says, hey, I don't think you should work this way.

380
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:25,340
I don't think this is how all of this should go down, but I'm going to wait.

381
00:25:25,340 --> 00:25:26,100
I'm going to listen.

382
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:29,320
I'm going to see what you might have to say about this.

383
00:25:29,320 --> 00:25:32,980
And God graciously and lovingly responds to Habakkuk.

384
00:25:32,980 --> 00:25:37,980
I think his response, too, is a two-part response.

385
00:25:37,980 --> 00:25:41,980
First thing I want to point out is in verse 4.

386
00:25:41,980 --> 00:25:45,420
He says back to Habakkuk, look at the proud.

387
00:25:45,500 --> 00:25:47,820
They trust in themselves, and their lives are crooked.

388
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:53,720
He says, but the righteous will live by their faithfulness to God.

389
00:25:53,720 --> 00:26:00,680
So Habakkuk's complaint is, how are you going to let injustice continue to happen?

390
00:26:00,680 --> 00:26:08,120
And God's response is, I'm going to let the righteous, the righteous will be those who live by faith.

391
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:20,400
This verse is quoted so many times in the New Testament to draw out this idea of sanctification by faith, salvation, justification by faith in Christ.

392
00:26:20,400 --> 00:26:25,260
And what God is saying to Habakkuk is, listen, injustice is injustice.

393
00:26:25,260 --> 00:26:28,560
Rebellion against God is rebellion against God.

394
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:32,720
Whether it's Israel's rebellion against God or Babylonians' rebellion against God.

395
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:36,600
Like, you look at it, and you see one sin worse than the other sin.

396
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:40,000
But the reality is, sin before God is sin before God.

397
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,780
And all sin before God will be punished.

398
00:26:42,780 --> 00:26:46,920
Some of it will be punished on the individual.

399
00:26:46,920 --> 00:26:53,420
And others of it will be punished on the head of Jesus, who will come and live a perfect life.

400
00:26:53,420 --> 00:26:57,640
And die a death that he does not deserve in our place for our sins.

401
00:26:57,860 --> 00:27:04,940
See, what happens with Christ on the cross is judgment for sin is being executed for those who will live by faith.

402
00:27:04,940 --> 00:27:15,060
Jesus, or God here is saying to Habakkuk, that rebellion against God is rebellion against God.

403
00:27:15,060 --> 00:27:18,940
And God ultimately will execute his judgment on an eternal timeline.

404
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:23,520
And you might not understand it, and it might not be the way that you would do it if you were in charge.

405
00:27:23,520 --> 00:27:25,340
But thank God you're not in charge.

406
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:33,800
Because God is a good God, a faithful God, a sovereign God who is going to execute his judgment on an eternal timeline.

407
00:27:33,800 --> 00:27:38,020
And that might not always comfort us in the midst of our struggle and our difficulty.

408
00:27:38,020 --> 00:27:41,020
And I don't think it necessarily comforts Habakkuk in the moment.

409
00:27:41,020 --> 00:27:47,780
It's this process where he's wrestling and asking questions and listening to God.

410
00:27:47,820 --> 00:27:53,000
And it's this process that our God invites us into when we struggle and when we have questions.

411
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:58,660
He invites us into this process where we wrestle with God and we wait and we listen.

412
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:04,740
We've got to be a people who learn to bring our requests to God and wait and listen.

413
00:28:04,740 --> 00:28:10,960
So the first part of God's response is, hey, all injustice is going to be judged one day.

414
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:18,040
The only righteous people, the only people who won't be judged are those who live by faith.

415
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:23,540
Whether they be Gentile or Jew, Babylonian or whatever it might be.

416
00:28:23,540 --> 00:28:29,160
Whoever places their faith and trust in me is the only people that will not be judged.

417
00:28:29,160 --> 00:28:31,660
And then he goes on in verse 14.

418
00:28:31,660 --> 00:28:33,660
And he says,

419
00:28:33,780 --> 00:28:42,560
For as the waters fill the sea, the earth will be filled with an awareness of the glory of the Lord.

420
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:48,700
I think the second part of God's response to Habakkuk is, justice is executed on an eternal timeline.

421
00:28:48,700 --> 00:28:59,440
He says, one day, there's going to come a day when my glory, my plan is going to so clearly fill the earth that it's going to be like the water covers the sea.

422
00:28:59,440 --> 00:29:01,500
I just got back from the beach.

423
00:29:01,500 --> 00:29:03,380
There's water everywhere.

424
00:29:03,780 --> 00:29:09,340
Like, like, I got a saying to Habakkuk, one day, my plans are going to be so clear.

425
00:29:09,340 --> 00:29:15,300
Like, right now, you look dimly, and you see part of my plans, and you can understand some of it.

426
00:29:15,300 --> 00:29:21,780
But there is going to come a day when I am going to send myself in humanity.

427
00:29:21,780 --> 00:29:25,720
That the word is going to become flesh and dwell among you.

428
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:33,760
And the plans, my plans, are going to be so clear that it's going to be as the water covers the sea.

429
00:29:33,780 --> 00:29:37,160
See, God gives us this deeply poetic picture.

430
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:43,140
And remember, when we talked about prophecy in Daniel, we said that all prophecy points to Jesus.

431
00:29:43,140 --> 00:29:51,000
And that the prophets, as they were writing these things, they were looking down this timeline, trying to figure out when it was that they were talking about.

432
00:29:51,880 --> 00:29:56,020
And theologians have coined this term called already not yet.

433
00:29:56,020 --> 00:30:07,440
And what this term means is that oftentimes prophecies about Jesus find partial fulfillment in his first coming, but ultimate fulfillment in his second coming.

434
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:13,060
And so I think this passage is both about the first coming and the second coming of Jesus.

435
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:24,500
That God's plans are ultimately going to be so clear to us that they fill the earth like the water covers the sea.

436
00:30:24,500 --> 00:30:30,320
The best way I could think to illustrate this is high tide and low tide.

437
00:30:31,780 --> 00:30:33,680
So I grew up, I love the beach.

438
00:30:33,680 --> 00:30:34,720
How many beach people do I have?

439
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:35,540
You guys beach people?

440
00:30:35,540 --> 00:30:36,840
A lot of beach people in here.

441
00:30:36,840 --> 00:30:39,820
I grew up, I love the beach, but we always went Thanksgiving week.

442
00:30:39,820 --> 00:30:40,800
That was like our tradition.

443
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:43,960
And it's pretty cold in South Carolina in November.

444
00:30:43,960 --> 00:30:48,040
So I love the beach, but I didn't spend a lot of time by the water.

445
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:49,680
We did the forestry things.

446
00:30:49,680 --> 00:30:52,440
Mini golf, lots of mini golf, all the mini golf.

447
00:30:54,200 --> 00:30:58,640
But when I got married to Marissa, she lives close to the shore, about an hour from the shore.

448
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:01,560
And so her parents are like beach people through and through.

449
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:06,660
And like when I think about the beach, it's like we spend an hour by the water and then we do a ton of fun things.

450
00:31:06,660 --> 00:31:13,940
Like her family, like they are by the water for like 12 hours a day, which I originally thought was really boring.

451
00:31:13,940 --> 00:31:15,920
I was like, what are we doing here?

452
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:17,700
Like we're just going to sit and watch the water.

453
00:31:17,700 --> 00:31:20,740
But they're like beach experts, and they taught me a couple of things.

454
00:31:20,740 --> 00:31:23,300
They taught me to bring a chair, which I didn't used to do.

455
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:25,580
It's uncomfortable to sit in the sand for 12 hours.

456
00:31:25,580 --> 00:31:26,440
Chairs help.

457
00:31:26,440 --> 00:31:28,180
They taught me to bring books.

458
00:31:28,180 --> 00:31:30,020
They taught me to bring like cornhole.

459
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:32,220
Like there's a lot of fun things you can do by the water.

460
00:31:32,220 --> 00:31:36,600
But the coolest thing that they taught me is like they know the tides.

461
00:31:36,600 --> 00:31:38,120
They know how the tides work.

462
00:31:38,120 --> 00:31:44,280
And so they'll set up their kind of permanent camp waiting for high tide to come in.

463
00:31:44,280 --> 00:31:52,400
But they'll take a chair or a towel down by low tide, and they'll kind of enjoy the water coming in.

464
00:31:52,560 --> 00:31:57,140
But they know for a fact where the tide is going to end up because they've been there for so long.

465
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:05,460
And man, as I was thinking about this passage this week, what a beautiful illustration of the life of the believer.

466
00:32:05,460 --> 00:32:10,200
Like the glory of the Lord is going to fill the earth like the water covers the sea.

467
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:13,020
And we have that low tide version of that right now.

468
00:32:13,020 --> 00:32:16,480
We get to live on the other side of the first coming of Jesus.

469
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:23,600
We understand the blessings of Jesus in our life, and we can enjoy them and experience them in the moment.

470
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:27,920
But we better be setting up camp waiting for high tide.

471
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:32,520
We better be living our life with an eternal focus, listening to God,

472
00:32:32,520 --> 00:32:36,180
and understanding that God's plans are executed on an eternal timeline.

473
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,820
Because let me be clear, the tide is coming in.

474
00:32:38,820 --> 00:32:40,220
Jesus will return.

475
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:47,160
And if we set up camp and live for the temporary, we're going to miss out on so many of the blessings of God.

476
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:53,180
I remember so many times sitting back as the tide's coming in, where we set up camp.

477
00:32:53,180 --> 00:32:57,920
And you see so many people scattering, people that aren't used to being at the beach, right?

478
00:32:57,920 --> 00:33:01,820
Because they're like, oh, shoot, like where we set up is about to be covered with water.

479
00:33:01,820 --> 00:33:05,520
And so they had to kind of ditch everything and find a new spot to set up.

480
00:33:07,020 --> 00:33:10,080
This is kind of how the Christian life is.

481
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:17,480
Like we know one day the knowledge of the glory of the Lord is going to fill the earth as the water covers the sea.

482
00:33:17,480 --> 00:33:20,080
So we live in anticipation for that day.

483
00:33:20,080 --> 00:33:24,100
We don't get distracted by the temporary blessings of this earth.

484
00:33:24,100 --> 00:33:24,920
We enjoy them.

485
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:28,400
We experience them in a good and a fresh way.

486
00:33:28,400 --> 00:33:36,040
But we set up camp waiting for God's eternal plans to be set up.

487
00:33:36,040 --> 00:33:37,920
This is God's response to Habakkuk.

488
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:39,160
Look, I know you don't get it.

489
00:33:39,160 --> 00:33:41,540
I know it seems harsh.

490
00:33:42,720 --> 00:33:45,400
I know it doesn't make sense to you where you're sitting.

491
00:33:45,400 --> 00:33:50,820
But if you'll just trust me, if you'll place your faith in me and step back and wait,

492
00:33:50,820 --> 00:33:54,760
I promise one day my plans are going to be made so clear to you.

493
00:33:54,760 --> 00:34:04,200
So Habakkuk says, God, how long are you going to let injustice prosper?

494
00:34:04,200 --> 00:34:08,820
How can you let a more unjust nation execute your judgment?

495
00:34:11,100 --> 00:34:14,880
And ultimately, God responds, hey, just trust me.

496
00:34:14,880 --> 00:34:16,420
I've got this thing.

497
00:34:16,420 --> 00:34:17,480
It's going to work out.

498
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:23,760
And this is this process that Habakkuk is going through with God,

499
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:29,100
where he's bringing his questions and his frustrations and his concerns and his doubts and his fear to God.

500
00:34:29,100 --> 00:34:33,240
And then God gives him the response.

501
00:34:33,240 --> 00:34:36,980
And what we have in chapter 3 is a song.

502
00:34:36,980 --> 00:34:39,900
Like Habakkuk sits down and writes a song.

503
00:34:39,900 --> 00:34:52,540
And I think the reason that that's important to us is because oftentimes I think we maybe we feel guilty for not being able to feel the same way that God feels about things in the moment.

504
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:55,500
Like God responds to Habakkuk.

505
00:34:55,500 --> 00:34:58,440
And I think we think we're supposed to just be like, okay, we got our answer.

506
00:34:58,440 --> 00:35:00,140
Now everything, like let's just move on.

507
00:35:00,140 --> 00:35:00,880
Everything's fine.

508
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:01,920
I can trust God now.

509
00:35:01,920 --> 00:35:11,060
And the reality is the fact that Habakkuk sat down and wrote a song is I think that it was this process where he wasn't just overnight like, okay, God, I get it.

510
00:35:11,060 --> 00:35:11,620
You're right.

511
00:35:11,620 --> 00:35:12,240
I'm wrong.

512
00:35:12,300 --> 00:35:13,380
I'm going to be happy now.

513
00:35:13,380 --> 00:35:18,300
It's like, no, like I'm going to engage in my relationship with you.

514
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:21,780
I'm going to wrestle with my questions and my doubts and my concerns.

515
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:30,640
And it's going to be a process before I can get to this point where I say, yet I will rejoice in the Lord.

516
00:35:30,640 --> 00:35:32,740
I will be joyful in the God of my salvation.

517
00:35:32,740 --> 00:35:38,880
If you're here and you're wrestling with God, maybe you've gotten some answers and you're like, I don't like these answers.

518
00:35:38,880 --> 00:35:40,140
Can we get some new answers?

519
00:35:40,140 --> 00:35:43,000
Like can we like hit the reset button and get a new answer?

520
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:44,800
Maybe you're here and that's you.

521
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:52,020
And I just want you to find comfort in the fact that like you don't have to just snap your fingers and have everything be okay.

522
00:35:52,020 --> 00:35:56,200
It's okay to wrestle with God, but do it in relationship with him.

523
00:35:56,600 --> 00:36:03,380
Like be intentional to bring those things to him and do it in relationship with other people who have faith and trust in God,

524
00:36:03,380 --> 00:36:06,940
who can encourage you and remind you of God's faithfulness.

525
00:36:06,940 --> 00:36:16,060
And so Habakkuk, he sits down and he processes what he's heard from God and ultimately he responds in a song.

526
00:36:16,060 --> 00:36:23,820
This is the longest intro to any message I've ever given because this is the main point that I hope we take home this morning.

527
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:35,340
That our faith to say yet in our present circumstances is based on God's eternal character, his past faithfulness, and his future promises.

528
00:36:35,340 --> 00:36:43,860
Like if we examine Habakkuk's song, he gets to a point where he can say in the present moment where he got answers that he didn't like.

529
00:36:43,860 --> 00:36:46,540
Things are happening in a way that he didn't want them to happen.

530
00:36:46,540 --> 00:36:57,320
And he's able to say no matter what happens, if this judgment happens from Babylon, if everything goes away, if all of the fields are empty,

531
00:36:57,320 --> 00:37:03,000
if all of the barns are empty, if we have no food, if we have no resources, yet I will rejoice.

532
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:04,640
He's able to say that.

533
00:37:04,640 --> 00:37:04,960
Why?

534
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:13,160
I think because he reflects on God's character, on his past faithfulness, and on his future promise.

535
00:37:13,160 --> 00:37:15,580
Look in verse 2 of chapter 3.

536
00:37:15,580 --> 00:37:17,640
He says,

537
00:37:17,640 --> 00:37:21,420
In your anger, remember mercy.

538
00:37:21,420 --> 00:37:25,220
In your anger, remember mercy.

539
00:37:25,220 --> 00:37:29,760
What Habakkuk is doing is he's quoting to God attributes of his character.

540
00:37:29,760 --> 00:37:32,900
He's saying, God, you are a merciful God.

541
00:37:32,900 --> 00:37:38,840
And so please, in your anger, choose to extend mercy to us.

542
00:37:38,840 --> 00:37:50,020
God's eternal character is one of the reasons that Habakkuk is able to say, yeah, in the midst of his process.

543
00:37:50,020 --> 00:37:55,580
And I'm a person that likes action.

544
00:37:56,300 --> 00:38:03,820
So my tendency when I read this passage in verse 3 is like, I just wanted to focus on God's past faithfulness and his future promises.

545
00:38:03,820 --> 00:38:13,080
But my wife, who is a worshiper, a meditator, a thinker, was like, no, it's not just about his actions.

546
00:38:13,080 --> 00:38:15,000
It's also about his character.

547
00:38:16,420 --> 00:38:18,760
Because sometimes all we have is his character.

548
00:38:18,760 --> 00:38:23,080
Like, some of us, like, we have, we're not seeing his actions.

549
00:38:23,080 --> 00:38:29,880
Like, if we look back at our past, if we think about our past, like, some of us don't have that.

550
00:38:32,620 --> 00:38:35,000
And even if we don't have that, we have his character.

551
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:37,520
He's a good God.

552
00:38:37,520 --> 00:38:40,320
He's a God of love, a God of mercy.

553
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:45,720
And God is the same yesterday, today, and forever.

554
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:49,960
God's character, his eternal character is good.

555
00:38:51,780 --> 00:39:00,320
So our faith to say, in the present, is based on who he is, like, his nature, his very character.

556
00:39:00,320 --> 00:39:11,940
But it's also, like, even if we don't have his past faithfulness in our situations, like, we have a history of God being faithful to the nation of Israel.

557
00:39:11,940 --> 00:39:15,120
All the way up to the point where he sent Jesus.

558
00:39:15,120 --> 00:39:16,240
And that's what Habakkuk does.

559
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:17,680
We don't have time to read through it.

560
00:39:17,680 --> 00:39:27,400
But chapter 3, the first 12 verses of chapter 3, this song that Habakkuk writes, he meditates on what God did in the past, his past faithfulness.

561
00:39:27,400 --> 00:39:31,260
He highlights how God delivered them out of Egypt through Moses.

562
00:39:31,260 --> 00:39:38,200
He highlights how God led his people in the wilderness through a cloud of, a pillar of fire and a cloud of smoke.

563
00:39:38,200 --> 00:39:42,420
He highlights how he parted the Red Sea in the Jordan River.

564
00:39:42,420 --> 00:39:48,560
He highlights how God caused the sun to stand still for Joshua and the nation of Israel.

565
00:39:48,560 --> 00:39:49,360
He's reflecting.

566
00:39:49,360 --> 00:39:56,420
He's saying, God, I don't understand what you're doing right now in my moment, in my story, in my life, but I know you've been faithful in the past.

567
00:39:56,420 --> 00:40:05,960
And because your eternal character is good, and because your track record of your faithfulness in the past is good, I'm going to cling to that.

568
00:40:05,960 --> 00:40:07,420
I'm going to hold tightly to that.

569
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:09,400
And I'm going to believe in that.

570
00:40:09,400 --> 00:40:22,880
And then I think he has in mind this future promise of Habakkuk 2, verse 14, that one day the glory of the Lord will fill the earth as the water covers the sea.

571
00:40:22,880 --> 00:40:28,200
He says, like, your character's good.

572
00:40:28,200 --> 00:40:31,060
You've been faithful in the past.

573
00:40:31,060 --> 00:40:33,660
And you've made a promise for the future.

574
00:40:34,540 --> 00:40:38,000
And so what happens in the present, it matters.

575
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:40,000
It makes me feel things.

576
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:49,880
But no matter what, I'm going to choose to say, yes, I will rejoice in the Lord.

577
00:40:49,880 --> 00:40:53,000
I will be joyful in the God of my salvation.

578
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:54,780
Why?

579
00:40:54,780 --> 00:40:57,220
Because I'm a person of faith.

580
00:40:57,220 --> 00:41:02,360
And chapter 2, verse 4 says that the righteous will live by faith.

581
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:09,860
I'm going to choose to believe, God, you're good, you've been good, and you're going to be good, even if I don't see it here in the present.

582
00:41:09,860 --> 00:41:12,040
Yet, I will rejoice in the Lord.

583
00:41:12,040 --> 00:41:16,640
I love how Psalm 103, verse 10 puts it.

584
00:41:16,640 --> 00:41:24,500
It says, God does not deal with us according to our sins, nor repay us according to our iniquities.

585
00:41:27,820 --> 00:41:28,540
We're sinful.

586
00:41:28,540 --> 00:41:30,200
We've broken God's law.

587
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:31,100
We deserve judgment.

588
00:41:31,100 --> 00:41:36,480
And any grace we get outside of that judgment is more than we deserve.

589
00:41:39,900 --> 00:41:52,100
And so even if the only answer to any of our questions, our frustrations, our concerns, our confusion, if the only answer we get when we come to God with our complaints is, I sent Jesus.

590
00:41:53,880 --> 00:41:55,380
It's more than we deserve.

591
00:41:55,380 --> 00:42:02,060
The only answer that we ever get to any of our questions is, Jesus is more than enough.

592
00:42:02,060 --> 00:42:07,160
Our faith to say, yet, in our present circumstances is based on God's eternal character.

593
00:42:07,160 --> 00:42:15,020
His past faith, once he sent Jesus the first time, and his future promises, we can cling right away to that fact.

594
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:20,160
Because because he sent Jesus the first time, we can have faith that he will send Jesus the second time.

595
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:27,080
And the glory of the Lord will fill the earth like the water covers the sea.

596
00:42:30,820 --> 00:42:39,200
Let's be a people that cling tightly to this process of, like, wrestle with God in the midst of our struggles.

597
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:45,240
Like, I think there's one of two ways we can go when we experience pain.

598
00:42:45,240 --> 00:42:52,840
The first way is, like, we can follow the example of Habakkuk, and we can do this process, this wrestling with God.

599
00:42:52,840 --> 00:42:55,200
Choose to be faithful in the process.

600
00:42:56,800 --> 00:43:01,340
But I think for so many of us, what we're tempted to do is have a faithless response.

601
00:43:01,340 --> 00:43:06,700
And the way I see that playing out in my own life, I'm just going to call myself out in front of you guys,

602
00:43:06,700 --> 00:43:09,200
and hopefully that will connect with you somewhere.

603
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:15,400
When I experience pain, when I'm not intentional to bring my pain to God in this process,

604
00:43:15,400 --> 00:43:19,540
it ends up with resignation.

605
00:43:19,540 --> 00:43:24,060
Where I'm angry, I have self-pity, depression.

606
00:43:24,860 --> 00:43:29,120
I'm like, you know what, if this is my lot in life, this stinks.

607
00:43:29,120 --> 00:43:32,260
And I don't want to deal with this right now.

608
00:43:32,260 --> 00:43:35,920
And it also leads to loneliness, because nobody wants to be around a person like that.

609
00:43:35,920 --> 00:43:39,120
Or maybe detachment, sometimes detachment.

610
00:43:39,120 --> 00:43:44,300
Like, I'm like, if this is my reality, rather than bringing it to God and asking him questions and wrestling with him,

611
00:43:44,300 --> 00:43:47,620
like, I'm just going to disconnect from my reality.

612
00:43:49,620 --> 00:44:01,860
Maybe I find a substance that numbs the pain, or I binge watch, or I go on social media black holes daydreaming about a life that isn't the life that I'm actually in.

613
00:44:04,760 --> 00:44:06,300
Or maybe it's none of those of you.

614
00:44:06,300 --> 00:44:07,160
Maybe it's bravado.

615
00:44:07,160 --> 00:44:16,560
Maybe you're a workaholic, and you generate this fake version of your life on social media for people to see,

616
00:44:16,560 --> 00:44:18,720
because you don't want to deal with their actual life.

617
00:44:18,720 --> 00:44:22,300
So you create this fake life that doesn't actually exist.

618
00:44:22,300 --> 00:44:26,300
You try and manipulate and manufacture your own life.

619
00:44:27,680 --> 00:44:28,820
These are faithless responses.

620
00:44:28,820 --> 00:44:33,600
This is what we do in bringing our constraints to God.

621
00:44:33,600 --> 00:44:35,840
I'm just going to help me understand.

622
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:47,480
I don't know where you're at this morning, but I think our goal as Christians is to get to a place where no matter what our present circumstances are,

623
00:44:47,480 --> 00:44:49,920
we can say, yeah, I will rejoice in the Lord.

624
00:44:49,920 --> 00:44:55,760
Hopefully, this is a message you can cling to around times like Thanksgiving, right?

625
00:44:56,520 --> 00:45:01,940
Whether things are great or things are bad, you can still say, I will rejoice in the Lord.

626
00:45:01,940 --> 00:45:06,240
I will be joyful in the God of my salvation.

627
00:45:06,240 --> 00:45:08,740
Our faith is say yet in our present circumstances.

628
00:45:08,740 --> 00:45:18,180
It's based on God's eternal character, who he is, his past faithfulness, what he's done, and his future promises, what he's going to do.

629
00:45:18,180 --> 00:45:24,020
Let's be a people that learn to rejoice in the Lord no matter what our circumstances are because of who God is.

630
00:45:24,680 --> 00:45:28,580
Because of what he's done, because of what we believe he's going to do with me.

631
00:45:28,580 --> 00:45:42,580
Father, we thank you so much for real people with real lives like Habakkuk, who brought his complaints to you, his concerns to you, his questions to you,

632
00:45:42,620 --> 00:45:51,760
who wrestled with you, who wrestled with you in doubt and frustration, and who heard a response from you that maybe he didn't necessarily like.

633
00:45:53,540 --> 00:46:06,580
But he was a person of faith who was willing to trust you, no matter what the present circumstances look like, because of who you are, because of what you've done, and because of what you're going to do.

634
00:46:06,580 --> 00:46:08,360
God, let us learn from his example.

635
00:46:08,360 --> 00:46:11,060
Let us be real people with real lives.

636
00:46:11,060 --> 00:46:14,580
Bring our struggles, our frustrations, our concerns, our questions, our doubts.

637
00:46:15,440 --> 00:46:17,200
Bring them all to you.

638
00:46:17,200 --> 00:46:22,840
Wrestle with what you might say in return.

639
00:46:22,840 --> 00:46:31,320
And no matter what we hear, God, let us be a people who are able to say, yet I will rejoice in the Lord.

640
00:46:31,320 --> 00:46:35,460
I will be joyful in the God of my salvation.

641
00:46:35,460 --> 00:46:37,400
In Jesus' name I pray.

642
00:46:37,400 --> 00:46:38,320
Amen.

643
00:46:38,320 --> 00:46:38,420
Amen.

644
00:46:38,420 --> 00:46:38,440
Amen.
